est mark of the year belonged to " [:a {:href "/athletes/1800000"} "Gretel Bergmann"] " of Germany, who had cleared 1.64 (5-4½) at the German Trials. But Bergmann was a Jew and the German Olympic Committee refused to place her on the team, and only two German “women” competed. One was Kaun, while the other was " [:a {:href "/athletes/70083"} "Dora Ratjen"] ", who placed fourth. However, after setting three world records in 1937 and 1938, Ratjen was revealed later that year to be a hermaphrodite – having mixed sexual characteristics. That seemed to be the end of it until the late 1950s, when Ratjen revealed “she” was really Heinrich Ratjen (listed for many years as Hermann Ratjen), and had been ordered by the Third Reich to compete as a woman to try to bring a gold medal to the Nazis."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Best Height Cleared"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.30 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.40 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.50 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.55 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.58 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.60 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.62 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.62 m (jump-off)"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.60 m (jump-off)"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "717"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/71035"} "Ibolya Csák"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "1.60"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "xo"] [:td {} "xxx"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "284"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/68745"} "Dorothy Odam"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: mi